  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1042 PM MST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\nSTORM SYSTEM MOVING ACROSS THE REGION THROUGH SATURDAY:\n\nLooking at upstream precipitation totals across California this\nAR was efficiently wrung out with feet of snow over the Sierras\nand some record rainfall rates set over the LA basin. Obviously\nthere was plenty of moisture to go around and strong southerly \nflow as pulled this plume across the Intermountain West. Showers\nblossomed over the western CWA through the afternoon/evening\nwith webcams showing impactful snow over the roadways of the\nUinta mountains. Models and the forecast remains on track with\nthe first lobe of PV energy rotating around the upstream low\nslated to move across the northwest CWA tonight. As expected the\nprecipitation has been convective/banded in nature including a \nmesoscale convergent band showing up downwind of the La Sals.\nReports of graupel over northwest Colorado is also not\nunexpected. The precipitation bands will begin to migrate\neastward toward sunrise as the main core of the jet and PV lobe\nrotates across the northwest CWA. This looks to push an ill\ndefined front through with thickness values decreasing through\nmid morning. There looks to be at least a small probability of a\nsnow squall threat over portions of NW Colorado including US40\nand I-70 tomorrow morning as this front interacts with \ninstability in place. This is a low confidence forecast but \nsomething to keep an eye on. Subsidence moving in behind the \npassing PV lobe looks to scour out the dendritic layer over much\nof the western CWA with showers staying focused over the\nColorado High Country and the high San Juans. The focus shifts \nto the SE CWA tomorrow night into early Saturday. The upstream\nenergy digs through the southern Great Basin carving out a\nmeridional trough that swings through the Rockies by mid-day on\nSaturday. This pushes a strong QG signal across our southern CWA\nproviding strong ascent but little moisture to work with.\nForecasts continue to keep most of the significant snow over the\nhigher peaks of the southern and central mountains where\ngenerally 2 to 5 inches will fall. There will likely be some\nhigher amounts reported especially if a precipitation band\nstalls but confidence is almost nil where this might occur. The\nFlat Tops and northern Park ranges look to pick up a bit more\nwith this event...4 to 8\" with locally higher but outside the\nhigher impact travel routes. That said winds will also be quite\nstrong in the pre and post frontal environment tonight into\nFriday morning and this will lead to blowing and drifting snow\nover the higher mountain corridors and travel will not be\npleasant at times. \n\n\nSUNDAY & BEYOND: \n\nThe pattern to start the holiday week looks to be progressive\nand active. Trend for the first storm continue to focus on the\nMonday/Monday night timeframe. Being transitory snow amounts\nare forecast to be light and mainly impact the divide areas.\nBehind this deterministic global model consensus falls off a \ncliff with GFS showing a compact Omega pattern over the southern\nCONUS and the EURO has a ridge out west and digging trough in\nthe central CONUS. Ensemble clusters would favor the EURO at\nthis point which means another quick moving system in the\nThursday timeframe and persistent NW flow keeping a threat of\nsnow over the northern mountains. Whether this plays out or not\nis far from certain. Continue to monitor the latest forecasts \nas model consensus improves on the timing of these waves. Daily \nhighs and lows will also trend roughly 5 degrees below seasonal \nnormals.",
